# SYNOPSIS

```usage
bus-acpi
```

# DESCRIPTION

`bus-acpi` runs ACPICA, the interpreter of the firmware's ACPI machine language, as a task of its own.
It loads the DSDT and SSDTs, runs their AML, reports the devices present to devmgr(8), which starts their drivers, and serves `/srv/acpi`, whose one request turns the machine off.

devmgr(8) starts it, once, after it has scanned PCI and started the PCI drivers; svcd(8) has no manifest for it.
It takes no arguments.
devmgr gives it:

: `acpi`
  The kernel's copy of every ACPI table, end to end, as one read-only VMO, with its size in the spawn message.
: `devmgr`
  A channel to devmgr, on which it asks for what its AML touches and reports the devices it finds.
: `listen`
  The server end of `/srv/acpi`, devmgr's `claim=acpi` (see svc(6)), when devmgr's manifest has one.
: `console`
  devmgr's console, where everything `bus-acpi` and ACPICA say goes.

and devmgr's own command line, which the kernel's is (devmgr's manifest says `cmdline`).
The only word of it `bus-acpi` reads is `bus-acpi.probe=1`; see {Probing|#probe}.

@node=tables title="The tables"

ACPICA finds tables through an RSDP and an XSDT at physical addresses, but the firmware's memory they were in may have been reclaimed since the kernel copied them.
So `bus-acpi` copies the kernel's copies into memory of its own and gives them addresses in a range no real table has, `0x7f0000000000` and up, behind an RSDP and an XSDT it makes, both named `VECTRA`.
ACPICA's mappings of that range are of the copies.

The XSDT lists every copy but the DSDT and any FACS.
The FADT's copy is changed to name the DSDT's copy and no FACS, and its checksum made again.
There is no FACS among the kernel's copies, so ACPICA runs without one (ADR-0030).

`bus-acpi` then initialises ACPICA fully: the tables loaded, the hardware put in ACPI mode with its fixed registers and event blocks set up, and the namespace's objects initialised, their `_INI` methods run.
Events are enabled but never delivered, since there is no SCI handler.

@node=regions title="What the AML reaches"

AML reaches hardware through operation regions: memory, I/O ports and PCI configuration space.
`bus-acpi` holds none of them to begin with.
When the AML first touches one, it asks devmgr for it on its `devmgr` channel and keeps what it is given, mapped, for the rest of its life:

- memory, as a physical VMO of the page-aligned range, mapped uncached and writable;
- I/O ports, on x86_64, as an IoRange mapped into its task; on aarch64 there are none, and every port access is refused;
- a PCI function's configuration space, its 4 KiB of segment 0's ECAM, as a physical VMO.

devmgr refuses RAM, the kernel's own devices (its interrupt controllers and IOMMU as the MADT, DMAR and IORT place them; on x86_64 the PIC's, the PIT's and the console's ports) and anything it has granted a driver, and says each answer on the console.
An access refused, or one past `bus-acpi`'s 64 memory ranges, 64 port ranges or 64 configuration spaces, fails in the AML with `AE_NOT_EXIST`, and `bus-acpi` says so once for each address.
The request is lib/vx-acpi/mint.h's `vx_acpi_mint`, by `channel_call` with a 5-second deadline.

@node=devices title="The devices"

`bus-acpi` walks the namespace for every device.
Each one present, by its `_STA` or by having none, with a hardware ID (`_HID`), is printed on the console with its path, its hardware ID and its `_CRS` resources: `io=`<base>`/`<count> for ports, `mem=`<base>`/`<size> for memory, `irq=`<line> for an interrupt.
Of an interrupt resource that offers a choice of lines, the first is taken.

Each is also reported to devmgr, by a `channel_write` of a `vx_acpi_device` with no reply: the hardware ID, the path (cut to 47 bytes) and up to 8 resources.
devmgr matches the hardware ID against its drivers' `match=acpi hid=` records and starts the driver it names with exactly those resources as grants, unless one is the kernel's or another driver's, as drv-rtc-cmos(3) for `PNP0B00`.

A last line counts the devices, those present with a hardware ID, and the KiB of `bus-acpi`'s 8 MiB heap in use.

@node=power title="/srv/acpi"

With a `listen` handle, `bus-acpi` says `serving /srv/acpi` and serves it; without one it serves nothing and waits for ever.
`/srv/acpi` is not a file server.
A client sends a request with `channel_call` on its connector: a `vx_msg_header` whose ordinal is `VX_ACPI_POWER_OFF` (lib/vx-acpi/mint.h); any other message is answered `INVALID`.

To power off, `bus-acpi` says `powering off`, waits a second so what the console holds is printed, then:

- where the firmware is not hardware-reduced and has an `_S5` object, it says `S5` and enters the sleep state S5 through ACPICA, as on x86_64;
- otherwise, or if the machine is still on after S5, it says `PSCI, through devmgr` and asks devmgr, which makes the kernel's `system_power` call (see power(2)): PSCI's `SYSTEM_OFF` by the FADT's conduit, as on QEMU's aarch64.

The reply comes only if the machine is still on, with the error in its `flags`.
poweroff(8) is the client.

@node=probe title="Probing"

With `bus-acpi.probe=1` on the command line, before loading the tables, `bus-acpi` asks devmgr for three things it must refuse: the interrupt controller's page (the local APIC at `0xfee00000` on x86_64, QEMU virt's GIC distributor at `0x8000000` on aarch64), a page of RAM, and the console's ports `0x3f8` to `0x3ff`, and says which it got.
The acpi scenarios check that all three are refused.

